Here's how to save and restore draft text using .NET MAUI's app lifecycle events and `Preferences`:

**In your `NotePage.xaml.cs`:**

```csharp
public partial class NotePage : ContentPage
{
    private const string DraftKey = "note_draft_text";

    public NotePage()
    {
        InitializeComponent();

        // Restore draft when page is created
        NoteEditor.Text = Preferences.Get(DraftKey, string.Empty);

        // Subscribe to lifecycle events
        App.Current.Windows[0].Deactivated += OnAppBackgrounded;
        App.Current.Windows[0].Activated += OnAppResumed;
    }

    private void OnAppBackgrounded(object sender, EventArgs e)
    {
        Preferences.Set(DraftKey, NoteEditor.Text ?? string.Empty);
    }

    private void OnAppResumed(object sender, EventArgs e)
    {
        // Optional: only restore if editor is empty (avoids overwriting)
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(NoteEditor.Text))
            NoteEditor.Text = Preferences.Get(DraftKey, string.Empty);
    }

    // Clean up when navigating away
    protected override void OnDisappearing()
    {
        base.OnDisappearing();
        App.Current.Windows[0].Deactivated -= OnAppBackgrounded;
        App.Current.Windows[0].Activated -= OnAppResumed;
    }

    // Call this after a successful save to clear the draft
    private void ClearDraft()
    {
        Preferences.Remove(DraftKey);
    }
}
```

**Key points:**

- **`Window.Deactivated`** fires when the app goes to background — persist the draft here.
- **`Window.Activated`** fires on resume.
- **`Preferences`** is ideal for small text; for large content, use `SecureStorage` or write to `FileSystem.AppDataDirectory`.
- Unsubscribe from events in `OnDisappearing` to avoid leaks.
- Call `ClearDraft()` after the user explicitly saves, so stale drafts don't reappear.
